<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dabhol</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dabhol is a historic port on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konkan-coast">Konkan coast</a> of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire</a>, ports such as Dabhol and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajapur">Rajapur</a> linked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a> trade networks. They handled the exchange of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> and the import of horses essential for cavalry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dabhol.jpg" alt="Dabhol" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nomaanmetkar · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dabhol_Graveyard_Mosque.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Dabhol, also written Dabul, is a small seaport town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ratnagiri">Ratnagiri</a> district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>. It lies on the northern and southern sides of the Vashishti River, which also flows by the town of Chiplun. Under the Bahmani dynasty the port was known as Mustafabad and later as Hamjabad before it took its present name. From the 14th to the 16th century it was the principal port of the southern Konkan, trading with ports of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean Sea</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Russian traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afanasy-nikitin">Afanasy Nikitin</a>, who visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from 1468 to 1474, found Dabhol a large town and extensive seaport, to which horses from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> were brought for sale, and which had links with all the major ports from India to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. In the 15th and 16th centuries it was an opulent Muslim trading centre, first under the Bahmani sultans and later under those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>. It was the port with the most convenient access to the Bahmani capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a>, and its fortunes rose with that dynasty; at its height it was arguably the most important port between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaul">Chaul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a>.</p><p>That prominence made it a target. In December 1508 a Portuguese expeditionary force under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-almeida">Francisco de Almeida</a> bombarded, sacked and razed the town in the Battle of Dabul, a prelude to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-diu-1509">Battle of Diu</a>, though the fort was not taken. The Portuguese tried several more times over the following decades to destroy it; at the last recorded attack, in 1571, the governor was Khwaja Ali Shirazi, and 150 men were killed at Dabhol.</p><p>The break-up of the Bahmani state into smaller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-sultanates">Deccan sultanates</a> hastened the port's decline. As new capitals were built its position was no longer so favourable, and during the 16th century much commerce moved south to the rising port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajapur">Rajapur</a>. Shivaji conquered Dabhol around 1660 and annexed it to the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> kingdom. It passed to Sambhaji, who gave it to Khando Ballal Chitnis as a reward for saving him from drowning in a river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Dabhol vatan is still claimed by descendants of the Chitnis family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satara">Satara</a>, although some descendants of the Shirke family argue that it should be theirs. On the southern side of the town, between the villages of Veldur and Ranavi, stands the Dabhol LNG power plant, which was set up by the company Enron.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dabhol” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom's western ports, such as Chaul and <b>Dabhol</b>, and after 1472 Goa, connected it to the Arabian Sea trade, importing horses from Arabia and Persia, which were essential for its cavalry, and exporting cloth and other goods.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The economy rested on the black cotton soils of the plateau, which produced cotton, millet and wheat, and on textile weaving and the trade of ports on the Konkan coast such as <b>Dabhol</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bijapur-sultanate">Bijapur Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Internal trade flowed along Deccan routes, while Konkan ports such as <b>Dabhol</b> linked the Marathas to the Arabian Sea trade in cotton, spices and the horses essential for cavalry.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
